import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="About">
  <div style="max-width:72ch">
    <h1>About this edition</h1>
    <p class="lede">An interactive, extensible edition of the chemistry in N. Ya. Turova's reaction-network tables, maintained as open, structured data.</p>

    <h2>Source</h2>
    <p>Natalia Ya. Turova, <i>Neorganicheskaya khimiya v tablitsakh</i> (Inorganic Chemistry in Tables), Higher Chemical College of the Russian Academy of Sciences,
      Moscow, 1999. The book presents, for each element, its compounds by oxidation state, with properties and structures, and the reactions that connect them,
      supported by about 5800 literature references.</p>
    <p>This site is <b>not</b> a reproduction of the book. The facts (compounds, numerical values, reactions and conditions) were extracted, checked against the
      printed pages, and restated in our own words and layout. Each value states its source. The literature cited by Turova is listed on every compound page.</p>

    <h2>How the data were made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The pages were scanned and transcribed region by region, then compared against an independent second reading and against automated chemistry checks
        (oxidation states from formulas, bond-length and melting/boiling-point plausibility, the book's own abbreviation list).</li>
      <li>Every block was checked manually against the scan.</li>
      <li>The verified text was converted into structured English records: one per compound and one per reaction. Turova's printed values are never overwritten.
        Corrections and newer values are added next to them, with a reference.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Reading the diagrams</h2>
    <p>As in the book, reactions are drawn as arrows: <span class="t-no_redox">solid</span> when the oxidation state does not change,
      <span class="t-oxidation">dashed</span> for oxidation and <span class="t-reduction">dash-dot</span> for reduction. The redox change may involve any element,
      e.g. a peroxide ligand. Temperatures are in °C, enthalpies of formation (ΔH°f, 298 K) in kJ/mol, distances in Å.
      Oxidation states marked <i>formal</i> are charge-balance values for metallic, nonstoichiometric or mixed-valence solids.</p>

    <h2>Editorial status</h2>
    <p>Points still under review (probable misprints in the original, ambiguous readings, values needing confirmation from modern literature) are listed under
      “Editorial notes” on the compound pages.</p>

    <h2>Contributing and licence</h2>
    <p>The data are kept as YAML files in a public repository. Corrections and literature updates are welcome as pull requests and are checked automatically before
      review. Data: CC BY 4.0. Code: MIT. Please cite this edition and the original book.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
